# IMPORTANT — ACF Field Renaming Risk

## The rule

**Changing an ACF field's Name (not Label) after real data exists is
equivalent to abandoning that field and starting a new one.**

- The **Label** is just what's shown in the wp-admin edit screen —
  changing it is always safe, has zero effect on stored data or on any
  PHP/query code.
- The **Name** is the literal key used to store and retrieve the value
  in WordPress's `wp_postmeta` table. There is no indirection — ACF's
  "Name" *is* the database key.

Renaming the Name field does not move, copy, or migrate the existing
stored value. The old data stays exactly where it was, under the old
key — but nothing looks for it there anymore, so it becomes invisible
(not deleted). `get_fields()`, `get_field()`, and any PHP referencing
the field by its old name will silently return nothing for that field,
on every existing post, with no error.

## Why there's no automatic fix

This isn't an ACF bug or a workflow gap — it's structural. WordPress's
postmeta table is a flat key-value store with no schema-migration or
rename-tracking mechanism. A "rename" is, at the database level,
indistinguishable from deleting one field and creating an unrelated new
one. Any custom-fields system built on top of WordPress has this same
constraint.

## The real risk: silent data loss, not just silent absence

If a post is **re-saved** while a field is in this "renamed but not
migrated" state, the edit screen will show the field as empty (since it's
now looking under the new name, finds nothing), and saving will write
*emptiness* to the new key — while the real value sits untouched, now
permanently orphaned, under the old key. The data isn't recovered by
this save; it's just left behind for good, unless someone specifically
thinks to check the old key with `wp post meta get` before that happens.

## Confirmed today

Renamed `event_end`'s field Name to `ends` (label change to "Ends" was
the actual intent). Result: Miranda Exhibition (event ID 22) showed no
end date at all in `events-schema-cli.sh` output — the value was still
present and recoverable:
```bash
docker exec -i oswp-wordpress-1 wp post meta get 22 event_end --allow-root
# → 2026-08-10 00:00:00
```
Confirmed genuinely still in the database, just no longer being read.
No post had been re-saved yet, so no data was actually lost — but this
was close to the destructive scenario described above.

## The fix, this time (revert)

Since the intent was only ever a label change, revert the Name back to
`event_end` in the ACF Local JSON file:
```bash
grep -n '"name": "ends"' wp-content/themes/twentytwentyfive/acf-json/group_6a606d82f3bb0.json
```
Change `"name": "ends"` back to `"name": "event_end"`. Keep
`"label": "Ends"` as-is — that was the correct, intended change.
`get_fields()` will immediately find the existing `event_end` postmeta
again on every post, no data migration needed, since nothing was ever
actually moved.

## The general fix, going forward (if a real rename is ever needed)

If a field's Name genuinely needs to change (not just its label), run a
one-time bulk migration immediately after the rename, before any post
gets re-saved:
```bash
docker exec -i oswp-wordpress-1 wp post list --post_type=event --field=ID --allow-root | \
while read id; do
  old_value=$(docker exec -i oswp-wordpress-1 wp post meta get "$id" OLD_FIELD_NAME --allow-root 2>/dev/null || echo "")
  if [ -n "$old_value" ]; then
    docker exec -i oswp-wordpress-1 wp post meta update "$id" NEW_FIELD_NAME "$old_value" --allow-root
  fi
done
```
Replace `OLD_FIELD_NAME`/`NEW_FIELD_NAME` accordingly. Run this once,
immediately, right after the rename — every post's data moves cleanly
to the new key, nothing orphaned, nothing lost.

## Standing rule

- Relabeling a field: always safe, do it via GUI or JSON, no follow-up
  needed.
- Renaming a field's actual Name on a schema that already has live
  data: **never do this without immediately running the migration
  script above.** Treat "rename the Name" and "run the migration" as
  one atomic action, always, no exceptions — never rename and move on
  without the follow-up step.
